calamares-settings-snapshot (0.1.3) unstable; urgency=low

  * partition.conf: add a partitionLayout with a 1 GiB unencrypted ext4
    /boot ahead of the root partition. Calamares formats LUKS2 with Argon2id,
    which Debian's GRUB 2.12 cannot unlock, so with /boot inside LUKS the
    installed system could not boot. GRUB now reads /boot directly and the
    initramfs asks for the passphrase once, through Plymouth.
  * partition.conf: offer swap only as a file inside the encrypted root
    (userSwapChoices none, file). The small and suspend choices made a
    second LUKS volume; with /boot unencrypted there is no keyfile, so the
    initramfs would have asked for the passphrase twice.
  * Add packages.conf and run the packages module after services-systemd,
    before initramfscfg. unpackfs clones the live squashfs, so the installed
    system kept live-boot, live-config, user-setup and the installer itself.
    They are now purged with apt (offline-safe for removals; update_db is
    set to false explicitly because the schema defaults it to true), and the
    final initramfs is built without the live-boot hooks.
  * Add /etc/xdg/autostart/snapshot-install-icon.desktop and
    /usr/bin/snapshot-install-icon, which put the Install SnapshOT launcher
    on the desktop at login, only in the live session, marked executable and
    trusted for Xfce (as calamares-settings-debian's
    add-calamares-desktop-icon does). It replaces the copy in /etc/skel,
    which every installed user inherited.
  * bootloader.conf: drop the legacy kernel and img keys. Calamares 3.3
    does not read them for GRUB and its schema rejects them.
  * Welcome page: show the lockup as the page banner (productBanner, 520 px
    wide) and collapse the "Welcome to the Calamares installer" label with the
    branding stylesheet, instead of text on top and the logo further down.
    productWelcome is dropped so the logo is not repeated under the checks.

 -- Blackbird Cyber <snapshot@example.invalid>  Thu, 01 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000

calamares-settings-snapshot (0.1.2) unstable; urgency=low

  * Add users.conf. Without it the users module logs "Using fallback groups",
    puts the user in lp/video/network/storage/wheel/audio and has no
    sudoersGroup, so the installed user cannot sudo. Groups follow
    calamares-settings-debian 13.0.13 minus lpadmin, scanner and bluetooth
    (no cups, sane or bluez in the image; Calamares would create them with
    user GIDs) plus dialout and kvm for the serial-console and qemu tools the
    image ships. nonempty and userGroup are dropped: Calamares 3.3 ignores
    both (minLength: 1 replaces nonempty).
  * Add mount.conf. With no extraMounts nothing is bind-mounted into the
    target, so grub-install and update-initramfs would run in a chroot
    without /dev, /proc, /sys or efivarfs.
  * Add fstab.conf with crypttabOptions luks,discard,keyscript=/bin/cat.
    Debian's cryptsetup-initramfs hook skips a root target whose crypttab key
    is a plain file, so with the module default the LUKS root would never be
    unlocked at boot; the keyscript form plus the encrypt_hook from
    initramfscfg is what calamares-settings-debian relies on.
  * Add welcome.conf: storage, RAM and root pre-flight checks and no internet
    check (offline install). It also puts requiredStorageGiB in global
    storage, without which the partition page never offers "replace" or
    "alongside".

 -- Blackbird Cyber <snapshot@example.invalid>  Thu, 01 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000

